        "What is still open": "What is still open",
        "What is true about this case that raised the marker, in one sentence.": "What is true about this case that raised the marker, in one sentence.",
        "What it does": "What it does",
        "What it does: warn shows the reader a notice and blocks nothing, refuse-publication refuses publishing a file on a case this party is on, refuse-send refuses an outbound message to this party.": "What it does: warn shows the reader a notice and blocks nothing, refuse-publication refuses publishing a file on a case this party is on, refuse-send refuses an outbound message to this party.",
        "What kind of act this case is, where the case type declares the facet.": "What kind of act this case is, where the case type declares the facet.",
        "What kind of answer this field holds. The list is OpenRegister's own property vocabulary, published at /apps/openregister/api/schemas/property-vocabulary and generated from the validator that checks the saved value, so the editor cannot offer a type the engine refuses. The last five values are the ones dossiq used to offer before the vocabulary reached the editor: 'date', 'url' and 'email' are a string with a format, 'enum' is a string with a choice list, and 'json' is an object. They stay accepted so a stored definition keeps working and keeps its value. Nothing rewrites them, the editor offers them only on the definition that already carries one, and each one names its replacement.": "What kind of answer this field holds. The list is OpenRegister's own property vocabulary, published at /apps/openregister/api/schemas/property-vocabulary and generated from the validator that checks the saved value, so the editor cannot offer a type the engine refuses. The last five values are the ones dossiq used to offer before the vocabulary reached the editor: 'date', 'url' and 'email' are a string with a format, 'enum' is a string with a choice list, and 'json' is an object. They stay accepted so a stored definition keeps working and keeps its value. Nothing rewrites them, the editor offers them only on the definition that already carries one, and each one names its replacement.",
        "What kind of thing moved: a document, a party or a task.": "What kind of thing moved: a document, a party or a task.",
        "What kind of wait this is. An applicant wait suspends the beslistermijn under Awb 4:5. A third-party wait is an advice or a document somebody else owes. An internal wait is ours and stops no statutory clock. A statutory wait is one the law itself imposes.": "What kind of wait this is. An applicant wait suspends the beslistermijn under Awb 4:5. A third-party wait is an advice or a document somebody else owes. An internal wait is ours and stops no statutory clock. A statutory wait is one the law itself imposes.",
        "What makes a case unread": "What makes a case unread",
        "What makes this status true. A status that declares conditions is DERIVED: the case moves into it as the conditions become true, and it leaves the list of moves a handler picks. A status that declares nothing is a judgment somebody makes, which is most of them. Every condition is written in the vocabulary OpenRegister's lifecycleCondition rule kind uses, so the evaluation is a deletion once that kind lands rather than a rewrite.": "What makes this status true. A status that declares conditions is DERIVED: the case moves into it as the conditions become true, and it leaves the list of moves a handler picks. A status that declares nothing is a judgment somebody makes, which is most of them. Every condition is written in the vocabulary OpenRegister's lifecycleCondition rule kind uses, so the evaluation is a deletion once that kind lands rather than a rewrite.",
        "What may I do right now": "What may I do right now",
